64-Bit C++ Member Ordering (Largest to Smallest)

1. 8-Byte Aligned Group (Top of Class/Struct):
    • Pointers & References: SpaceEntity*, Client*, SystemBubble*, PyServiceMgr&, function pointers (8 bytes)
    • 8-Byte Primitives: double, int64, uint64, long, size_t (8 bytes)
    • Math & Engine Structs with double/int64: Vector3d (24 bytes), WarpState (80 bytes), SystemData, SystemKillData
    • STL Containers & Smart Pointers: std::map (48 bytes), std::vector (24 bytes), std::string (8 bytes on GCC 4.9.2 libstdc++), std::list, InventoryItemRef / SolarSystemRef (8 bytes)
2. 4-Byte Aligned Group:
    • 4-Byte Primitives: float, int32, uint32, standard enum (4 bytes)
    • 4-Byte Aligned Structs: Timer (16 bytes, 4-byte aligned)
3. 2-Byte Aligned Group:
    • 2-Byte Primitives: int16, uint16, short (2 bytes)
4. 1-Byte Aligned Group (Bottom of Class/Struct):
    • 1-Byte Primitives: bool, uint8, int8, char, enum : uint8 (1 byte)

⚡ Quick Rules of Thumb

• Constructor Initializer Lists:
Always keep your .cpp constructor initializer list in the exact same top-to-bottom order as the .h
declarations to avoid GCC -Wreorder warnings.
• Plain bool Over : 1 Bit-Fields:
Stick with plain bool members at the bottom unless you're packing 8+ flags into a hot struct
plain bool avoids bitwise read-modify-write instructions on GCC 4.9.2.
• Nested Structs:
A nested struct aligns to its largest internal member
e.g., WarpState contains int64, so it belongs in the 8-byte group;
Timer contains uint32, so it belongs in the 4-byte group

zero-initalize structs and "pragma pack(1)" using <id> <name> = {};"
